# This repository is PUBLIC (a hackathon rule). Nothing secret may ever land in
# it, and nothing may be copied in from the private Bizik repo without being
# read first. The entries below are the floor, not the whole safeguard.
node_modules/
dist/
coverage/

# Secrets. Real env files never, example files always - Bizik ships
# deploy/.env.production.example and a plain `!.env.example` negation would
# not rescue it, because the negation matches the basename only.
.env
.env.*
!*.example

# Local scratch
*.log
.DS_Store
tmp/
scratch/

# Demo media can be large; keep generated output out of git.
demo/output/

# ---- Markdown stays out of git --------------------------------------------
# Not deleted - the files stay on disk and are still ours to read. They are
# simply never committed to a PUBLIC repository.
#
# This matters more than it first looks. bizik/docs/ holds genuinely internal
# material: compliance-audit.md and processor-register.md cite our own audit
# findings by severity (H-4, M-4), incident-response.md is an operational
# runbook, DEPLOYMENT.md describes the infrastructure, and the WhatsApp and
# WasenderAPI guides carry live setup identifiers. Publishing a list of your
# own unresolved findings is a map for anyone who wants to use them.
#
# Our own planning notes go too: docs/HACKATHON.md carries prize targets, the
# track decision and a cut list, and docs/BUILD-PLAN.md is strategy. None of
# that belongs in front of the people judging us, or our competitors.
*.md

# The ONE exception, and only at the root. The rules make "failure to clearly
# identify your original contributions" an immediate disqualification, and the
# README is where that identification lives: which files predate the hackathon
# and which were written inside it. A public repo with no README answers that
# question with silence. The leading slash keeps it to this file - bizik's own
# READMEs stay ignored.
!/README.md
